      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense, thanks for correcting that. i think i see what you mean about being cautious with the wording. \n\none more thing, though—when i'm looking at the 'plausible fluctuations' or the random error itself, does the statistical community have a specific way of conceptualizing those errors? like, is there a standard way to think about those unexpected 'hiccups' or outliers that mess with the data, or do people just treat them as unexplained mathematical artifacts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The statistical community has various ways to conceptualize and handle unexpected errors or outliers. One common approach is to acknowledge the concept of sampling variability or random noise. Many researchers adopt the notion that any observed effect is likely due to a combination of the systematic effect of interest (the coefficient or estimated relationship) and residual variability. This implies that effect size is constrained by the natural variability present in the data.\n\nIn your discussion, you can discuss this aspect by framing the residual variability as \"small enough to be considered negligible\" or \"within the realm of expected random fluctuation\" when spanning a wide confidence interval. This way, you can convey a sense of resolving and accepting the potential role of error while avoiding vague claims of uncertainty. Many researchers use ordinal language like \"overlapping plausible ranges\" or \"confidence coexisting with uncertainty\" to describe the coexistence of expected results and unwarranted deviations from those expectations.",
